    "title": "When does Artemis II land? Navy divers ready for recovery",
    "text": "NASA’s Artemis II crew has completed its primary mission objectives and is now preparing to return to Earth later this week. (Photo by Chip Somodevilla/Getty Images)Chip Somodevilla/Getty ImagesNASA’s Artemis II crew has completed its primary mission objectives and is set to return to Earth in a matter of hours. The Artemis II mission set a new record for the farthest human spaceflight at 6:07 p.m. CT Monday, reaching 252,760 miles from Earth. The crew broke the previous record of 248,655 miles set by Apollo 13 earlier that day.Article continues below this adGet Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.Get Digital Access and Stay Informed With Trusted Local News.ONLY 25¢“It is blowing my mind what you can see with the naked eye from the moon right now. It is just unbelievable,” Canadian astronaut Jeremy Hansen radioed. He added that future missions should ensure “this record is not long-lived.”The 10-day mission will conclude Friday with a planned splashdown off the California coast. NASA expects the crew to land in the Pacific Ocean near San Diego at 5:07 p.m. PDT (7:07 p.m. CT) Friday.Here’s what to know about the return schedule.When does Artemis II return to Earth?Artemis II’s lunar flyby and observation period lasted about seven hours, a key milestone in the nearly 10-day test flight. The mission will end with a splashdown Friday, April 10, in the Pacific Ocean.Article continues below this adNASA says the spacecraft is following a “free-return” lunar trajectory — a path that uses the gravity of Earth and the moon to guide Orion back while minimizing fuel use.Artemis II return scheduleHere is the planned timeline for April 10 (all times CT):Article continues below this ad 2:05 a.m.: Crew sleep begins 10:35 a.m.: Flight Day 10 crew wakeup 12:50 p.m.: Orion cabin configuration for re-entry begins 1:53 p.m.: Return trajectory correction burn 5:30 p.m.: NASA+ coverage begins 6:33 p.m.: Crew module and service module separation 6:37 p.m.: Crew module raise burn 6:53 p.m.: Entry interface 7:07 p.m.: Splashdown in the Pacific Ocean 9:30 p.m.: Post-splashdown news conference at NASA Johnson Space Center How the Navy will help Artemis IIAccording to a Navy press release, the amphibious transport dock USS John P. Murtha (LPD 26) is scheduled to act as the recovery vessel for the Orion spacecraft and its crew when they return from the historic Artemis II mission.Article continues below this adThe spacecraft is scheduled to splash down off the coast of San Diego at approximately 7:07 p.m. CT (5:07 p.m. PDT). After being recovered by the USS John P. Murtha, the crew will undergo medical evaluations in the ship’s infirmary before heading back to shore and flying to NASA’s Johnson Space Center in Houston.“The U.S. Navy’s amphibious transport dock has unique advantages, including a well deck, helicopter pad, onboard medical facilities, and communication capabilities needed to support the mission,” Petty Officer 1st Class Jomark Almazan said. “The platform gives NASA the ability to recover the Orion space capsule and collect critical data to help ensure it’s ready to recover the astronauts and capsule during future Artemis missions.”Stories you won't want to miss, handpicked by Statesman editors.April 10, 2026Marley Malenfant is a 10-year multimedia journalist who’s worked in print, digital, and broadcast. His prior work includes working in West Texas as a TV reporter for KTXS. During his time there, he covered education, sports, features, and breaking News. He’s skilled in front of the camera as well as behind it. He was a freelance writer, published in D Magazine, Dallas Morning News, SB Nation, Dallas Observer, and was a personality for “The Coach Brothers” sports radio show. Outside of journalism, he was an educator in the Dallas area. He was a 2012 Mayborn Literary Nonfiction Conference grant recipient. In 2021, he was part of the NABJ student multimedia project. He graduated with a paralegal degree from El Centro in 2023. Currently, he’s a trending news reporter for the Austin American-Statesman, covering breaking News, sports, music, and legal trends.",
